WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:10.094
Bridgett and I recently took a trip to 
Rochester, New York.

00:00:10.094 --> 00:00:13.359
To recreate a photo from 1872.

00:00:13.866 --> 00:00:15.099
Alright.

00:00:15.099 --> 00:00:17.626
Turn just a little bit – that’s it, 
just like that.

00:00:17.626 --> 00:00:19.073
Only your eyes, look here.

00:00:19.073 --> 00:00:20.073
Okay.

00:00:20.073 --> 00:00:22.185
It’s very strange where she’s looking.

00:00:22.185 --> 00:00:23.477
This is Mark Osterman.

00:00:23.477 --> 00:00:27.312
I was the process historian at the 
George  Eastman Museum for 21 years.

00:00:27.312 --> 00:00:30.320
My specialty is understanding 
the technical evolution

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:34.953
of photography from its inception 
all the way up to about 1900.

00:00:34.953 --> 00:00:37.590
He and his wife, France, have been 
teaching and practicing

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:40.926
historic photo processes for over 30 years.

00:00:40.926 --> 00:00:44.392
Using the original reference manuals 
from the 1800s,

00:00:44.392 --> 00:00:47.660
the same chemicals, 
and the same cameras.

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:49.310
Starting...

00:00:49.310 --> 00:00:50.483
now.

00:00:50.483 --> 00:00:53.329
The photo we’re here to recreate 
is this one

00:00:53.329 --> 00:00:55.678
of the widowed Mary Todd Lincoln

00:00:55.678 --> 00:01:00.000
with the ghost of her dead husband,
US President Abraham Lincoln.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.030
Ghost is going to look slightly toward me
with the head down.

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:04.053
That’s it.

00:01:04.053 --> 00:01:07.175
The image is the most famous work 
of William Mumler

00:01:07.175 --> 00:01:12.000
who claimed his camera could photograph 
the invisible spirits of dead loved ones

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.377
was driven to the edge of ruin 
in a sensational criminal trial

00:01:16.377 --> 00:01:17.858
but got away with it.

00:01:17.858 --> 00:01:21.720
Because no one could figure out 
exactly how Mumler did it.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:23.410
So, we tried to.

00:01:26.845 --> 00:01:31.992
In the mid-1800s, technological breakthroughs
like photography and the telegraph

00:01:31.992 --> 00:01:35.691
were seen as something sort of... supernatural.

00:01:35.691 --> 00:01:38.909
For the first time, people could see 
true-to-life depictions

00:01:38.909 --> 00:01:41.380
of loved ones who weren’t there with them.

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:45.788
And receive messages from hundreds
of miles away, instantaneously.

00:01:45.788 --> 00:01:51.298
So it was this sense that technology was shrinking
the distance between people.

00:01:51.298 --> 00:01:54.561
And so every time there was 
a new technology introduced during 

00:01:54.561 --> 00:01:56.312
this moment in American history

00:01:56.312 --> 00:01:58.691
it had spiritual repercussions.

00:01:58.691 --> 00:02:03.875
Peter Manseau is a scholar of American religious
history and author of several books.

00:02:03.875 --> 00:02:07.909
Including one about how photography’s rise
in the mid-1800s

00:02:07.909 --> 00:02:13.149
coincided with a new religious movement gaining
ground in the US and Europe:

00:02:13.149 --> 00:02:14.238
Spiritualism.

00:02:14.238 --> 00:02:19.251
The principle belief of Spiritualism was that
contact with the dead was possible

00:02:19.251 --> 00:02:20.858
if you had the right means.

00:02:20.858 --> 00:02:23.219
That connection was made through a “medium,”

00:02:23.219 --> 00:02:26.488
a person who claimed they could communicate
with the dead.

00:02:26.488 --> 00:02:30.900
From the beginning, many people suspected
spiritualist mediums to be frauds.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:36.157
Still, the movement grew to include thousands
— and, by some accounts, millions

00:02:36.157 --> 00:02:38.549
of Americans in the late 19th century.

00:02:38.549 --> 00:02:41.859
Including high-profile people, 
like Mary Todd Lincoln

00:02:41.859 --> 00:02:47.678
who turned to spiritualism to help process
the death of her son Willie, in 1862.

00:02:47.678 --> 00:02:50.000
During this period in American history,

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.252
the need to reach the dead 
became more critical than ever.

00:02:53.252 --> 00:02:55.189
With the coming of the Civil War

00:02:55.189 --> 00:03:00.536
and with casualties on an order that Americans
had never experienced before,

00:03:00.536 --> 00:03:04.608
this hunger to communicate with 
the spirit world really took off.

00:03:04.608 --> 00:03:08.299
So when an unknown amateur photographer 
in Boston

00:03:08.299 --> 00:03:12.944
began developing photos that apparently included
apparitions of the dead

00:03:12.944 --> 00:03:15.532
it attracted the attention of the Spiritualists.

00:03:15.532 --> 00:03:19.719
William Mumler, an engraver by trade, had
been tinkering with photography

00:03:19.719 --> 00:03:26.278
in a studio owned by his eventual wife, Hannah:
a spiritualist medium and professional photographer.

00:03:26.278 --> 00:03:30.079
It seems to be the case that Hannah Stuart
taught William Mumler photography.

00:03:30.079 --> 00:03:34.469
According to Hannah, William accidentally
captured the spirit of his dead cousin

00:03:34.469 --> 00:03:36.841
while taking a practice self-portrait

00:03:36.841 --> 00:03:41.020
at a time he said he was alone 
in the studio in 1862.

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:44.180
And he soon was mobbed with 
Spiritualists in Boston

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:46.870
who were eager to see if 
he could do this again.

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:51.898
And, as it turns out, he could – always
with Hannah nearby.

00:03:51.898 --> 00:03:54.054
She was the experienced photographer here.

00:03:54.054 --> 00:03:58.034
Mumler became the public face, but she knew
what she was doing.

00:03:58.034 --> 00:04:02.609
The Mumlers took photo after photo that, miraculously,

00:04:02.609 --> 00:04:07.479
revealed that a spirit of a lost loved one,
or sometimes a total stranger

00:04:07.479 --> 00:04:09.583
was in the room with the sitter.

00:04:09.583 --> 00:04:13.959
Even though the Mumlers’ spirit forms were
usually faint and out of focus

00:04:13.959 --> 00:04:18.899
people coming in for portraits often reported
that they recognized the spirits in the photos

00:04:18.899 --> 00:04:22.275
as the very family members they were hoping
to connect with.

00:04:22.275 --> 00:04:26.982
Spirit photography seemed to be the next level
of connection to the afterlife.

00:04:26.982 --> 00:04:30.613
You no longer needed to be there in the presence
of the medium.

00:04:30.613 --> 00:04:35.420
You could take these photographs with you
anywhere and say, “See, this is real.”

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:37.540
And like all things Spiritualists did

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:40.619
spirit photography brought its fair share
of skeptics

00:04:40.619 --> 00:04:43.347
and people trying to figure out the trick.

00:04:43.347 --> 00:04:46.340
You have a lot of people from the 
photographic community in Boston 

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:48.049
who were seeking out his services

00:04:48.049 --> 00:04:51.353
specifically to show that he was not 
who he said he was.

00:04:51.353 --> 00:04:56.070
A variety of them went and tried to follow
him through the full photographic process.

00:04:56.070 --> 00:05:00.244
And no one could ever figure out exactly what
he was doing and how he was doing it.

00:05:00.244 --> 00:05:04.617
But enough accusations of fakery in Boston
eventually prompted the Mumlers

00:05:04.617 --> 00:05:06.805
to shut the studio down and relocate.

00:05:06.805 --> 00:05:11.389
Mumler arrives in New York several years after
he became notorious in Boston.

00:05:11.389 --> 00:05:15.630
Maybe enough time had elapsed that his name
would no longer mean anything.

00:05:15.630 --> 00:05:20.523
New York City at this time was where all the
top American photographers and galleries were.

00:05:20.523 --> 00:05:26.097
And, starting in 1869, where a spirit photographer
from Boston had opened up shop.

00:05:26.097 --> 00:05:28.965
And he was really only operating there 
for a matter of months

00:05:28.965 --> 00:05:31.396
before he ran afoul with the law.

00:05:31.396 --> 00:05:34.759
Mumler was arrested on fraud charges 
and thrown in jail.

00:05:34.759 --> 00:05:37.230
His trial was written up in New York newspapers

00:05:37.230 --> 00:05:40.000
and appeared on the front page 
of Harper’s Weekly

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:43.300
which called it “remarkable and 
without precedent."

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:47.440
He was just beloved by the media because 
he was such a colorful character

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:51.030
in making such outrageous claims – 
that just made great copy.

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:53.270
The trial was kind of a circus.

00:05:53.270 --> 00:05:55.273
The prosecution brought in P.T. Barnum

00:05:55.273 --> 00:05:58.370
of Barnum &amp; Bailey’s “Greatest Show 
on Earth” fame

00:05:58.370 --> 00:06:02.548
as a key witness and self-described 
“expert of humbug.”

00:06:02.548 --> 00:06:05.290
P.T. Barnum always had a problem 
with Spiritualists

00:06:05.290 --> 00:06:07.834
because he felt that they were 
taking advantage of grief

00:06:07.834 --> 00:06:09.779
and he felt that it was going too far.

00:06:09.779 --> 00:06:13.738
So he made it very clear that he was happy to
see Mumler caught and to see him go down.

00:06:13.738 --> 00:06:16.851
But there was one small problem for the prosecution.

00:06:16.851 --> 00:06:21.323
None of those experts, all of whom followed
Mumler through his process

00:06:21.323 --> 00:06:25.680
none of them could say, “This is exactly
what Mumler did, and I saw him do it.”

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:29.476
The prosecution listed 9 different ways 
Mumler  might have done his trick.

00:06:29.476 --> 00:06:34.036
Some of them pretty elaborate, like placing
a “microscopic picture of the spirit”

00:06:34.036 --> 00:06:36.530
inside the camera through a screw hole.

00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:41.470
Or a secret light that impressed the ghost
onto the glass plate when it’s coated with

00:06:41.470 --> 00:06:44.175
photosensitive chemicals inside this tank.

00:06:44.175 --> 00:06:48.816
They argued that his photos must be explainable
by at least one of the methods.

00:06:48.816 --> 00:06:51.470
And that Hannah Mumler was somehow involved

00:06:51.470 --> 00:06:53.243
possibly as a distraction.

00:06:54.883 --> 00:06:58.360
One of the simplest ways 
to get a spirit photo then and now

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:01.880
is a technique called 
“Sir David Brewster’s ghost”

00:07:01.880 --> 00:07:05.539
where the “spirit” is only in frame 
for a few seconds

00:07:05.539 --> 00:07:09.037
while the main sitter holds still 
for the rest of the exposure.

00:07:09.037 --> 00:07:11.176
In our case, nearly a full minute.

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:20.000
Leaving a faint ghostly form behind the sitter.

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:20.934
And we did that.

00:07:20.934 --> 00:07:24.736
We did the easy way first, just to show 
how that can be done.

00:07:24.736 --> 00:07:29.031
But this can’t be how Mumler did it in front
of skeptical photographers

00:07:29.031 --> 00:07:30.541
watching his every move.

00:07:30.541 --> 00:07:34.980
If you're going to try to show this as real
spirit photography in front of witnesses

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:37.910
you can't have somebody dressed up as a ghost
and walk in.

00:07:37.910 --> 00:07:42.720
Even though the prosecution demonstrated that
spirit photos can be made through trick photography

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:45.260
they couldn't confidently identify 
Mumler's method

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:48.626
and therefore failed to prove 
beyond reasonable doubt 

00:07:48.626 --> 00:07:51.266
that his pictures were not of spiritual origin.

00:07:51.266 --> 00:07:54.201
And Mumler was acquitted.

00:07:54.201 --> 00:07:56.047
So how did he do it?

00:07:56.047 --> 00:08:00.989
Mark thinks he has an answer and agreed 
to try his theory out with Bridgett and me.

00:08:00.989 --> 00:08:04.180
First, Mark took a regular portrait 
with no ghost

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:06.903
the kind Mumler would have been observed doing.

00:08:06.903 --> 00:08:11.588
Then, he carefully outlined my position on
the ground glass of the camera.

00:08:11.588 --> 00:08:16.360
Bridgett dressed in a white suit, and we covered
her face and hands with white powder

00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:19.727
and Mark created a separate negative 
of the ghost.

00:08:21.899 --> 00:08:26.423
Lining it up so the hands landed in the right
place: on my shoulders.

00:08:26.423 --> 00:08:29.210
And you can see the parts of it have been
scraped away

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:30.680
so that when I put them together

00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:33.611
they're exactly in the place 
I want them to be.

00:08:33.611 --> 00:08:37.878
But it’s the next step that’s the most
crucial to this potential method.

00:08:37.878 --> 00:08:41.668
Because it's in the printing where he's making
the magic happen.

00:08:41.668 --> 00:08:46.818
Mumler – like most portrait photographers
at the time – printed his photos on albumen.

00:08:46.818 --> 00:08:50.410
Specially treated paper, that, when placed
against the glass negative

00:08:50.410 --> 00:08:55.424
in this printing box and exposed to light
results in a positive print.

00:08:55.424 --> 00:08:58.629
Mark thinks that, after passing the original
negative around

00:08:58.629 --> 00:09:02.620
to show witnesses there was no trickery 
with a bit of sleight of hand

00:09:02.620 --> 00:09:06.538
Mumler could have slipped the ghost negative
underneath the portrait negative.

00:09:06.538 --> 00:09:10.623
Then in front of their eyes you then put it
into the printing frame like so.

00:09:10.623 --> 00:09:15.399
As long as I keep that against something dark,
you don't see the secondary ghost negative

00:09:15.399 --> 00:09:18.949
because white will photograph as dark 
on the negative

00:09:18.949 --> 00:09:22.265
which will then be white on the final print.

00:09:22.265 --> 00:09:25.164
When Mumler opens it up to show the final
positive print

00:09:25.851 --> 00:09:27.091
the ghost is there.

00:09:28.244 --> 00:09:31.399
With this potential method, the Mumlers could
have had a collection

00:09:31.399 --> 00:09:35.923
of already-made “spirit” negatives, and
could line up the sitter with a “ghost”

00:09:35.923 --> 00:09:39.620
that matched the general appearance 
of their lost loved one.

00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:41.581
It should be noted that Mark’s technique

00:09:41.581 --> 00:09:45.205
concealing one negative and printing 
the two of them simultaneously

00:09:45.205 --> 00:09:49.881
isn’t listed as one of the 9 methods the
prosecution against Mumler came up with.

00:09:49.881 --> 00:09:52.408
Though the prosecution was 
probably right in saying 

00:09:52.408 --> 00:09:54.760
that the Mumlers used several different methods

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:58.646
going with whichever process 
was least capable of detection.

00:09:58.646 --> 00:10:02.211
That 1869 Harper’s Weekly story 
about Mumler’s trial 

00:10:02.211 --> 00:10:05.000
predicted that now that he’d 
gotten away with it

00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:07.350
he was probably going to 
keep taking spirit photographs

00:10:07.350 --> 00:10:12.339
pointing out that “he has been prosecuted,
and thus extensively advertised.”

00:10:12.339 --> 00:10:13.470
And they were right.

00:10:13.470 --> 00:10:17.094
In fact, the Mumlers made what is arguably
their most celebrated work 

00:10:17.094 --> 00:10:20.108
after the trial, 
from 1869 onward

00:10:20.108 --> 00:10:23.611
including this one of Mary
Todd Lincoln in 1872.

00:10:23.611 --> 00:10:29.012
And this one of prominent abolitionist 
William Lloyd Garrison in 1874.

00:10:29.012 --> 00:10:33.461
Mumler even ran regular ads in the spiritualist
newspaper “Banner of Light"

00:10:33.461 --> 00:10:38.639
for a mail-in service in which a client could
send a photo of themselves to the Mumlers

00:10:38.639 --> 00:10:43.620
and receive a photo of the photo, 
plus the presence of a spirit.

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:47.800
Because for the people that did believe, 
like Mary Todd Lincoln

00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:51.591
the spirits they saw in these images 
brought them a unique form of comfort.

00:10:51.591 --> 00:10:55.997
As far as she was concerned, it was proof
that her late husband was still there 

00:10:55.997 --> 00:10:57.471
still a presence in her life.

00:10:57.471 --> 00:11:00.000
Proof of something that she always believed.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:04.009
Mumler insisted for the rest of his life 
that his photos were authentic

00:11:04.009 --> 00:11:06.519
and proved the existence of an afterlife.

00:11:06.519 --> 00:11:09.509
In the opening line of his memoir, 
published in five parts

00:11:09.509 --> 00:11:13.927
in “Banner of Light” in 1875, he wrote
that his spirit photographs

00:11:13.927 --> 00:11:17.174
"contributed evidences of a future existence”

00:11:17.174 --> 00:11:20.129
And, though persecuted, he was satisfied

00:11:20.129 --> 00:11:22.082
at having been “a humble instrument 

00:11:22.082 --> 00:11:25.529
in the hands of the invisible host 
that surrounds us.”

00:11:26.426 --> 00:11:28.445
If you want to read first hand accounts

00:11:28.445 --> 00:11:31.247
news reports from the 1860s 
about spirit photography 

00:11:31.247 --> 00:11:32.423
and Mumler's memoirs:

00:11:32.423 --> 00:11:35.476
the best and most often cited resource
for all things Mumler 

00:11:35.476 --> 00:11:40.957
is this book, The Strange Case of William Mumler, 
Spirit Photographer, by Louis Kaplan

00:11:40.957 --> 00:11:42.957
who I also interviewed for this episode. 

00:11:42.957 --> 00:11:47.441
As more researchers are digging into 
Mumler's sort of murky story 

00:11:47.441 --> 00:11:49.441
one thing is starting to become clear: 

00:11:49.441 --> 00:11:55.563
that Hannah Mumler, aka Helen F. Stuart,
is likely the mastermind behind the man

00:11:55.563 --> 00:11:58.823
and has been credited as the true inventor 
of spirit photography

00:11:58.823 --> 00:12:02.655
in this fascinating research paper
written by Felicity Tsering Chödron Hamer.

00:12:02.655 --> 00:12:06.327
Both of those resources and more
are linked in the description of this video.

00:12:06.327 --> 00:12:09.495
And this is the final episode 
of Darkroom season 3!

00:12:09.495 --> 00:12:12.125
This season took us to a lot of
amazing places

00:12:12.125 --> 00:12:15.183
and I want to give a huge thanks
to all of our sources

00:12:15.183 --> 00:12:19.679
who generously gave us their time 
and knowledge to help bring you these stories.

00:12:19.679 --> 00:12:22.057
You can watch every Darkroom episode 
from this season

00:12:22.057 --> 00:12:24.572
and the first two season in the full playlist

00:12:24.572 --> 00:12:26.572
which is also linked in the description.

00:12:26.572 --> 00:12:28.572
Thanks for watching
and see you next time. 

